In fact, pretty much all the Roman emperors that were familiar with today are the ones who left behind something monumental. Because that was the whole point of building the monuments in the first place. The Romans were always thinking about the future. They were always imagining us, 2,000 years down the road, marveling at what they created. As soon as they can afford to build an expensive stone, they do build an expensive stone because they know it will last. and they're always producing for the generations in the future to make sure they will be remembered. The most expensive, durable, monumental stone to build with was marble, and Romans quarried it from all over their empire. They got green marble from Greece and red porphyry from Egypt.
事实上，今天我们熟悉的几乎所有罗马皇帝，都是那些留下了某种宏伟建筑的人。因为这正是当初建造纪念碑的全部意义所在。罗马人总是在考虑未来。他们总是在想象我们，在两千年后，惊叹于他们创造的东西。一旦他们有能力建造昂贵的石头建筑，他们就会建造昂贵的石头建筑，因为他们知道这会持久，而且他们总是在为未来的世代生产，以确保他们会被人们记住。最昂贵、最耐用、最宏伟的建筑石材是大理石，罗马人从他们帝国的各地开采大理石。他们从希腊获取绿色大理石，从埃及获取红色斑岩。

