00:13:17.000 --> 00:13:55.000
sculptor has gone in and subtly reshaped the face because he's got quite a distinctive head, Constantine. So they have made his big square head and put that onto the original. This effectively made it look like Constantine with his big square head was doing all the stuff that past emperors had done, like riding a horse over the bodies of his enemies, or addressing a crowd of troops, or hunting a lion. The idea was to remind people of when Rome was great. To invoke a memory of Rome at its healthiest as its most prosperous.
雕刻师走进去，巧妙地重塑了面部，因为君士坦丁的脑袋相当独特。所以他们做出了他那个巨大的方形脑袋，并把它安到了原作上。这实际上使得看起来像是拥有巨大方形脑袋的君士坦丁在做着过去皇帝所做的一切事情，比如骑马踩过敌人的尸体、向军队发表讲话，或者猎狮。这个想法是为了提醒人们罗马伟大的时期。唤起人们对罗马最健康、最繁荣时期的记忆。

28
00:13:55.000 --> 00:14:16.000
So it is a specific and deliberate choice to make that clear connection back to a kind of lost golden past. Constantine's art was a masterpiece of political messaging, and it was also a turning point in the history of architectural reuse. There has been some debate among scholars
因此，这是一种具体而刻意的选择，旨在与某种失去的黄金时代建立清晰的联系。君士坦丁的艺术是政治信息的杰作，也是建筑重复利用历史上的一个转折点。学者们一直在争论

29
00:14:16.000 --> 00:14:54.000
about whether the repurposing of the panels was artistic or just practical, but a lot of them think it wasn't just recycling to cut costs. It was meant to communicate something about the new political moment. A column pulled out of a pagan temple still has an aura of prestige. A carved panel from Rome's golden age still carries a certain authority. Constantine understood that if you placed those materials in a new context, all of that accumulated meaning could be deployed. in new ways. It was reuse as propaganda. Constantine's reuse also set a new precedent.
关于嵌板的重新利用是出于艺术考虑还是仅仅出于实用，但许多人认为这不仅仅是为了削减成本而进行的回收利用。它是为了传达关于新的政治时刻的一些东西。从异教神庙中拆出来的柱子仍然带有声望的光环。来自罗马黄金时代的雕刻嵌板仍然承载着某种权威。君士坦丁明白，如果你把这些材料放在一个新的语境中，所有那些累积的意义就可以被部署。以新的方式。这就是作为宣传的重复利用。君士坦丁的重复利用也树立了一个新的先例。

30
00:14:54.000 --> 00:15:34.000
Once the emperor himself started pulling apart old monuments to build new ones, it gave permission to the rest of the empire. It became this interesting and legitimate thing to do. And soon this kind of reuse was spreading. People started salvaging old columns and decorative freezes and ornamental stonework. They would pull. pull that material out of old buildings and use it in new construction. And a lot of that new construction was churches. Constantine had legalized Christianity, and now the faith needed a lot of grand new buildings to match its status as a legitimate religion.
一旦皇帝本人开始拆解旧纪念碑来建造新纪念碑，这就为帝国其余的人开了绿灯。这成了一件有趣且合法的事情。很快，这种重复利用就开始蔓延。人们开始打捞旧柱子、装饰性质地饰带和观赏性石雕。他们会拉。把这些材料从旧建筑中拉出来，用于新建设中。其中许多新建设都是教堂。君士坦丁将基督教合法化，现在这个信仰需要大量宏伟的新建筑来与其作为合法宗教的地位相匹配。

31
00:15:34.000 --> 00:16:13.000
Huge basilicas went up across the empire, and they needed marble. Thankfully, there were crumbling pagan temples everywhere, full of the kinds of stone that would make churches look amazing. And it becomes about the power of the church on earth to show that this is God's earth and we can decorate his house with all of the beautiful things. The other thing that supercharged this new wave of architectural reuse was that Constantine had founded a new capital named for himself in Constantinople. And much of the new building in Constantinople involved materials salvaged from other places.
巨大的巴西利卡大教堂在整个帝国拔地而起，它们需要大理石。谢天谢地，到处都是摇摇欲坠的异教神庙，里面充满了能让教堂看起来令人惊叹的石头。这变成了关于地球上教会的力量，以表明这是上帝的土地，我们可以用所有美丽的东西来装饰他的另一个家。推动这股新的建筑重复利用热潮的另一件事是，君士坦丁在君士坦丁堡建立了一个以他自己名字命名的首都。而君士坦丁堡的大部分新建筑都涉及从其他地方打捞来的材料。

32
00:16:13.000 --> 00:16:46.000
When he founds Constantinople, he sweeps around. the empire taking up cool stuff from other places, basically, in order to decorate his new city, much to the stress of everybody else. A lot of people were not thrilled about the old marble temples being pulled apart. Especially in Rome, it sometimes felt like the city was being taken apart piece by piece. Everything that we know about what ordinary people saw about it is that they hated it. People actually rioted. They were so fed up that their neighborhoods were starting to look rampant.
当他建立君士坦丁堡时，他在。整个帝国到处搜刮其他地方的好东西，基本上是为了装饰他的新城市，这让其他人感到非常紧张。许多人对古老大理石神庙被拆散并不感到兴奋。特别是在罗马，有时感觉这座城市正在一块一块地被拆解。我们所知道的关于普通人对此的看法的一切是，他们恨透了。人们甚至发生了骚乱。他们太厌烦了，因为他们的街区开始显得破烂不堪。

33
00:16:46.000 --> 00:17:15.000
shackle and just bad. Like that was a nice building that was in my neighborhood and it had really nice columns on it and now it doesn't have any columns and the roof is falling down. And what nobody's going to do anything about that. They needed the equivalent of a Roman HOA, some enforcer who might keep up the standards of the neighborhood. And eventually, about 50 years after Constantine basically invented this kind of reuse, later emperors were passing laws trying to stop it.
破烂而且糟糕。就像那是我街区里的一栋漂亮的建筑，上面有非常漂亮的柱子，而现在没有任何柱子了，屋顶正在倒塌。而且没有人会对此做任何事情。他们需要一个相当于罗马物业管理委员会（HOA）的机构，某种能够维持街区标准的执法者。最终，在大约君士坦丁基本上发明这种重复利用的50年后，后来的皇帝通过法律试图阻止它。

34
00:17:15.000 --> 00:17:50.000
If you were caught stealing stone from buildings, you could be fined. But the laws were pretty toothless, and it kept happening anyway. And this tension never really went away. There were the people who wanted to preserve the old buildings. And then there were the people who wanted to strip them for parts. In fact, that tension eventually became embedded in the word that we use for the practice. The term spolia was coined during the Renaissance, when researchers, of the time had rediscovered classical Rome and fallen in love with it.
如果你被抓到从建筑物中偷石头，你可能会被罚款。但这些法律相当无牙，而且这种事情还是不断发生。这种紧张关系从未真正消失。有一部分人想要保护旧建筑。还有一部分人想要剥离它们的零件。事实上，这种紧张关系最终融入了我们用来形容这种做法的词汇中。术语spolia是在文艺复兴时期创造的，当时的研究人员

35
00:17:50.000 --> 00:18:35.000
And when they noticed that beautiful ancient Roman stonework had been salvaged and then wedged into medieval church walls, they were disgusted. It looked to them like the church had pillaged the ancient world. They coined the word spolia from the Latin for the spoils of war. To describe this process, to make it into a process of war of almost attack on these old buildings. The use of Spolia starts as a renaissance moral judgment on what has happened. But over time, the term lost its moral sting. And now in academic writing, it is just used as a neutral descriptor. Spolia outlived the empire that made it famous.
当时的学者重新发现了古典罗马并深深爱上了它。当他们注意到美丽的古罗马石雕被抢救出来并塞进中世纪的教堂墙壁时，他们感到恶心。在他们看来，这就像是教会掠夺了古代世界。他们从拉丁语中创造了“spolia”一词，意为战利品。用来描述这个过程，将其变成一个战争的过程，几乎是对这些旧建筑的攻击。Spolia的使用始于文艺复兴时期对所发生事情的道德判断。但随着时间的推移，这个词失去了它的道德刺痛感。现在在学术写作中，它只被用作一个中性的描述词。Spolia的寿命超过了使其闻名的帝国。

36
00:18:35.000 --> 00:19:19.000
Wherever the Romans built stuff, later civilizations found their materials and put them to new uses. Roman tiles showed up in medieval churches in England. Charlemagne installed Roman stonework in the chapel in his palace. And across the Islamic world, mosques were built with columns salvaged from Roman temples. But Spolia is really just one expression of a much older and more universal human instinct to reuse. After the Second World War, for example, when European cities lay in ruins, people picked through the rubble and used what they found to rebuild. That wasn't really spolia because it wasn't about symbolism.
无论罗马人在哪里建造东西，后来的文明都会找到他们的材料并将其用于新的用途。罗马瓷砖出现在英格兰的中世纪教堂中。查理曼大帝在他的宫殿小教堂里安装了罗马石雕。在整个伊斯兰世界，清真寺是用从罗马神庙中打捞出来的柱子建造的。但Spolia其实只是人类更古老、更普遍的重复利用本能的一种表达。例如，第二次世界大战后，当欧洲城市成了一片废墟时，人们在瓦砾中挑选并利用他们发现的东西进行重建。那其实不是spolia，因为它与象征意义无关。

37
00:19:19.000 --> 00:19:52.000
It was just necessity. But still, the underlying logic was the same. For most of human history, reuse wasn't some special thing. It was just how people built. But it's not how people build anymore. That's when we come back. Today, the vast majority of construction involves demolishing what's already there and starting from scratch, with all new materials that are uniform and predictable,
那只是出于必需。但尽管如此，其背后的逻辑是一样的。在人类历史的大部分时间里，重复利用并不是什么特殊的事情。这只是人们建造的方式。但这已经不是人们现在的建造方式了。这就是我们回归的时候。今天，绝大多数建筑都涉及到拆除现有的东西并从头开始，使用全新、统一且可预测的材料，

38
00:19:52.000 --> 00:20:16.000
with no history and no variability. It's often cheaper and more efficient than reusing the old stuff. But now, a growing number of architects and researchers think that reuse is a practice we need to relearn. Because it turns out that making everything from scratch with raw materials is something the planet can't sustain. As we look into the future,
没有历史，也没有可变性。它通常比重复利用旧东西更便宜、更高效。但现在，越来越多的建筑师和研究人员认为，重复利用是我们必须重新学习的一种做法。因为事实证明，用原材料从头制造一切是地球无法承受的。当我们展望未来时，

39
00:20:16.000 --> 00:20:48.000
we're not going to be in an extract and throw away world. This is Kate Semenon, a professor of architecture at the University of Washington. I mean, by requirement, we're going to need to reuse the things that we have made previously. Otherwise, ultimately, we would run out. The labor and fuel expended in creating even a single brick are enormous. And Kate says that it's worth sitting with what it actually takes to make something new. So in order to make a brick, you have to excavate the clay material from the earth.
我们将不再处于一个开采和抛弃的世界。这是华盛顿大学建筑学教授凯特·西门农（Kate Simonen）。我的意思是，根据要求，我们需要重复利用我们以前制造的东西。否则，最终我们会耗尽。制造哪怕一块砖所消耗的劳动力和燃料都是巨大的。凯特说，值得坐下来好好想想制造一样新东西到底需要付出什么。所以在制作一块砖时，你必须从地球上开采黏土材料。

40
00:20:48.000 --> 00:21:13.000
And in doing that, you're using an excavator that's burning deep. And so as you're burning that diesel, emissions go up into the atmosphere. So that's step one, just digging the clay out of the ground. Then you have to shape it. And that's often done with a machine. And that machine might be using electricity, making that electricity has emissions. Then the brick needs to be cured. And that usually means burning natural gas to fire the kiln.
在这样做时，你会使用一台深度燃烧的挖掘机。因此，当你燃烧柴油时，排放物就会进入大气层。所以这是第一步，仅仅是从地下挖出黏土。然后你必须塑造它。这通常是由机器完成的。那台机器可能会使用电力，而产生这种电力会有排放。然后这块砖需要固化。这通常意味着燃烧天然气来烧窑。

41
00:21:13.000 --> 00:21:46.000
And that natural gas then releases emissions into the atmosphere. And finally, you have to transport the brick to wherever it's needed, which requires a truck or a train. train or an airplane. And that then is also, again, likely burning diesel, which releases emissions. Now consider every brick, every steel beam, every panel of drywall, and every pane of glass in every building in the world. There's just an enormous amount of materials and labor and what's called embodied carbon in
然后这些天然气就会向大气中释放排放物。最后，你必须把这块砖运输到任何需要它的地方，这需要卡车、火车。或飞机。然后，这也同样可能会燃烧柴油，从而释放排放物。现在想想世界上每栋建筑中的每一块砖、每一根钢梁、每一块干墙板和每一块玻璃。有大量的材料、劳动力以及所谓的隐含碳存在于

42
00:21:46.000 --> 00:22:18.000
the built environment. Which is why a growing number of architects and engineers argued that we basically need to practice spolia at scale. And that sounds like a great idea, but it's actually very hard to do. When you salvage something like an old steel beam, it's hard to know exactly what you've got, like what it's actually made of and what loads it can carry. There is no reliable supply chain for reclaimed materials, at least not at the scale we really need.
建筑环境中。这就是为什么越来越多的建筑师和工程师认为，我们基本上需要在规模上实践spolia。这听起来是个好主意，但实际上很难做到。当你打捞像旧钢梁这样的东西时，很难确切知道你得到了什么，比如它到底是用什么材料制成的，能承受什么荷载。回收材料没有可靠的供应链，至少在我们真正需要的规模上没有。

43
00:22:18.000 --> 00:23:02.000
And most buildings aren't designed to be taken apart. It takes a lot of time and effort to deconstruct an old building piece by piece so that the parts can be used again. How we make buildings today has an influence on how easy they will be to take a part in the future. And not that many building owners today are asking their design teams to make it easy to tear down my building in 60 to 80 years. That's not really their top priority. In other words, it's hard to get out of that emperor mindset that buildings must last forever. There are some traditions that better understand a building's transience. For example, Japanese temples used to be built without nails or screws.
而且大多数建筑都不是为了被拆解而设计的。把一栋旧建筑一块一块地拆解以使零件可以再次使用，需要花费大量的时间和精力。我们今天建造建筑物的方式会影响它们未来拆解的容易程度。如今并没有那么多的建筑业主要求他们的设计团队让我的建筑在60到80年后更容易拆除。这并不是他们的首要任务。换句话说，很难摆脱那种建筑必须永存的皇帝心态。有一些传统更好地理解了建筑的短暂性。例如，日本寺庙过去常常不用钉子或螺丝建造。

44
00:23:02.000 --> 00:23:42.000
Instead, they were supported by interlocking wooden joints so that they could be taken apart and reassembled. And there are modern examples, too, like the Olympic Village during the 2024 Paris Games that was designed. to be converted to housing after the competition. So there are buildings here and there being designed with their next life in mind. I asked Kate what she thought about the Roman connection, how they took their crumbling buildings and used them as raw materials for something new. I think those buildings actually had fallen apart. You know, they were the debris of previous civilizations.
相反，它们由互锁的木质接头支撑，以便可以拆卸和重新组装。也有现代的例子，比如2024年巴黎奥运会期间的奥运村，它的设计目标就是在比赛结束后改造成住房。因此，这里和那里都有一些在设计时就考虑了其下一个生命周期的建筑。我问凯特她对罗马连接的看法，他们如何将崩塌的建筑用作新事物的原材料。我认为那些建筑其实已经倒塌了。你知道的，它们是前代文明的残骸。

45
00:23:42.000 --> 00:24:16.000
And so ultimately, our lives will be the debris for other civilizations. There are a lot of ways to think about what makes building materials valuable. Kate thinks about it in terms of scarcity. The earth is finite. There's not an endless amount of clay, sand, and stone. We have to be careful not to exhaust the materials we have. The Roman emperors thought about value in terms of prestige. Placing old materials in new contexts allowed them to communicate something about continuity and power.
因此，归根结底，我们的生活将成为其他文明的残骸。有很多方法可以思考是什么让建筑材料变得有价值。凯特从稀缺性的角度来考虑这个问题。地球是有限的。黏土、沙子和石头的数量并不是无穷无尽的。我们必须小心，不要耗尽我们拥有的材料。罗马皇帝从声望的角度来思考价值。将旧材料置于新的语境中使他们能够传达关于连续性和权力的信息。

46
00:24:16.000 --> 00:24:47.000
And the residents of Granby in South Liverpool measured value in a different way. They cared about the history of their neighborhood. And when they worked with Lewis Jones and the team at Assemble, they didn't just see old and dilapidated houses. They saw the raw materials for something new. Granby Rock, the product they made from the neighborhood's debris, was a way of insisting that the community still had worth. It was important to put these things into the houses,
利物浦南部格兰比的居民以另一种方式衡量价值。他们关心他们街区的历史。当他们与路易斯·琼斯和Assemble团队合作时，他们看到的不仅仅是破旧的老房子。他们看到了创造新事物的原材料。“格兰比岩”，这个他们用街区废墟制造的产品，是一种坚持认为这个社区仍然具有价值的方式。把这些东西放进房子里很重要，

47
00:24:47.000 --> 00:25:40.000
that would express the, you know, yeah, love and care and attention that had gone into renovating them. But since the early days of the Granby project, Lewis and his collaborators have started thinking much more about reuse in the context of climate change. The Granby Workshop didn't close when the renovation was finished. Instead, it grew into something bigger, a manufacturing studio in the neighborhood that's still running today. It produces architectural tiles and ceramics out of recycled materials. They fire these tiles at lower temperatures to reduce their environmental footprint, and they use electric kilns. So what started as spolia in the oldest sense has evolved into something more like what Kate Semenin advocates for. The workshop now helps other projects turn their construction debris into new building materials.
这将表达出，你知道的，是的，对翻新这些房子所投入的爱、关怀和关注。但自从格兰比项目的早期阶段以来，路易斯和他的合作者开始更多地从气候变化的背景来思考重复利用。当翻新完成时，格兰比工作室并没有关闭。相反，它发展成了更大的东西，一个至今仍在运营的街区制造工作室。它用回收材料生产建筑瓷砖和陶瓷。他们以较低的温度烧制这些瓷砖以减少环境足迹，并且他们使用电窑。因此，最初在最古老意义上的spolia已经演变成类似于凯特·西门农所倡导的东西。该工作室现在帮助其他项目将其建筑废料变成新的建筑材料。

48
00:25:40.000 --> 00:26:23.000
It's reuse that involves symbolism, but it's also a practical recognition that we can't keep extracting raw materials forever. Every project always starts with trying to understand what's already there and how to be really resourceful. And so reuse is a big, big part of that. I asked Lewis what Granby looks like today. More than a decade after Assembly joined with the community land trust to renovate the first houses. Granby today still very much has the same spirit. I think it had, you know, when I first visited 15-odd years ago, there's still a kind of sense of wildness there.
这是一种涉及象征意义的重复利用，但它也是一种实用的认可，即我们不能永远开采原材料。每个项目总是从试图理解已经存在的东西以及如何真正足智多谋开始的。因此，重复利用是其中非常非常重要的一部分。我问路易斯今天的格兰比是什么样子的。在Assemble加入社区土地信托翻新第一批房子十多年后，今天的格兰比仍然很有那种精神。我认为它有，你知道的，当我15年前第一次来访时，那里仍然有一种野性。

49
00:26:23.000 --> 00:27:09.000
You know, there's still, like, furniture on the street, there's plants in the pavement, there's the odd painting on the wall, whatever it is. But obviously, you know, amongst that, there are a lot more people living now. Lewis described kids playing on the streets, where not too long ago every house had one of those signs. The ones that said all items of value have been removed from this property. Those signs were, of course, wrong. The materials of the past are never worthless.
你知道的，街上仍然有家具，路面上有植物，墙上有奇怪的画，不管是什么。但显然，你知道的，在这其中，现在有更多的人住在那里了。路易斯描述了孩子们在街上玩耍的情景，不久前，每栋房子都挂着那些牌子。那些写着“本物业所有有价值的物品均已被搬走”的牌子。这些牌子当然是错的。过去的材料永远不是一文不值的。

50
